Research move (not on a PR; first step of "find THE candidate" follow-up to PR #350).

Question: take SM charged-fermion mass² values as the GIVEN spectrum;
ask whether they sit on a Fukushima-Shima SG decimation orbit R(λ) = λ(5 − 4λ).

Three complementary tests:

1. Per-generation orbit fit — for each family (leptons, up-quarks, down-quarks),
   find (scale s, integer k_μ, integer k_τ) such that R^{k_μ}(m_light²/s) ≈ m_mid²/s
   AND R^{k_τ}(m_light²/s) ≈ m_heavy²/s. Best residual 1.06 (down-quarks); worst 1.99
   (up-quarks, top mass overshoots R's [0, 5/4] domain). All families fit poorly.

2. Cross-family consistency — best-fit decimation depths come out as:
     k_μ = [7, 5, 5] across (leptons, up-quarks, down-quarks)
     k_τ = [11, 7, 11]
   No common level structure. If SG-family decimation governed flavour, these
   should match.

3. log₅ hierarchy check — at small λ, R(λ) ≈ 5λ, so integer decimation levels
   imply log₅(mass² ratio) ≈ integer. Mean integer-deviation across 6 ratios:
   0.267 (random baseline: 0.25). The up-quark sector is the cleanest hit
   (mid/light dev 0.076, heavy/mid dev 0.105) but leptons are far off
   (dev 0.375 and 0.493). Not coherent across SM.

VERDICT: The Fukushima-Shima SG R(λ) = λ(5 − 4λ) CANNOT reproduce the SM
mass hierarchy on a single decimation orbit. This refutes the whole
SG-family candidate space — sharply narrows future research.

Next research moves (separate spikes):
  - Sweep alternative R(λ) polynomials (degree 3+, different branching)
  - Hexagonal-SG / pentagasket families (PR #350 commit A candidates,
    re-tested with inverse method)
  - Non-polynomial decimation maps (transcendental fractals)
  - Drop integer-level assumption: continuous-time renormalisation flow
    on the SM mass operator

Files:
  - docs/srmech/notes/sm_inverse_decimation_spike_script.py (NEW)
  - docs/srmech/notes/sm-inverse-decimation-spike-per-test-2026-05-12.ndjson (NEW)
  - docs/srmech/notes/sm-inverse-decimation-spike-2026-05-12.png (NEW)

Per [[user_explanation_discipline]]: inverse direction (working backwards
from the SM spectrum to derive geometry constraints) is the sharp tool;
forward sweeps (PR #350 commit D) tell you whether ANY config in a
finite catalogue fits, but inverse tells you which whole FAMILIES of
fractals are ruled out.

